        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Uterus</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151982,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are no distinctive clinical features.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151983,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mixed endometrial carcinomas are uncommon {26132201}. In one case series, patient age was in the sixth decade of life {39922938}. Tumours of mixed histological types are sometimes enriched for dMMR and POLE {28114191; 39922938; 32205482}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151984,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151985,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mixed endometrial carcinomas typically represent divergent differentiation or progression from low-grade endometrioid carcinoma {30550482; 25677978; 26492180; 32604171}; rarely they may represent biologically unrelated collision tumours. Usually, the tumour exhibits the molecular features of each component, but there may be some shared features and some degree of molecular ambiguity {25677978}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151986,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151987,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>At least two spatially distinct histological types of endometrial carcinoma must be identified by histology. Immunohistochemistry can demonstrate biomarker differences between both types of carcinomas. This diagnostic category should not be used for morphological variations of endometrioid, clear cell, or serous carcinoma or for carcinoma with ambiguous morphology, dedifferentiated carcinoma, or carcinosarcoma. The most commonly encountered admixture is of endometrioid and serous carcinoma. Any percentage of high-grade/aggressive carcinoma that can be confidently demonstrated is sufficient to label the tumour as a mixed endometrial carcinoma, because even a small percentage of serous, clear cell, or mesonephric-like carcinoma may confer an adverse prognosis and outcome {31318970; 20393071}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151988,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 1098,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151989,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1098,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1085,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 1101,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151990,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1101,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> endometrial carcinoma with two distinct histological types; at least one component is either serous, clear cell, or mesonephric-like; exclusion of dedifferentiated carcinoma and carcinosarcoma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> immunohistochemical demonstration of the two distinct carcinoma types.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1083,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 1099,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151991,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1099,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>This entity is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of tumours of the uterus&nbsp;&ndash; endometrium</em> [[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1084,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 1100,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 151992,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 83,
        "headingId": 1100,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The behaviour of these tumours is dictated by the highest-grade component {31318970; 33622922}. Therefore, these tumours are graded as high-grade carcinoma irrespective of the relative percentages of serous or clear cell carcinoma present. There is controversy about whether mixed carcinomas have a better prognosis than pure clear cell and serous carcinomas {28114191; 33423806; 37634258; 37081760}. It has been reported that dMMR and POLE mixed carcinomas have better prognosis {28114191; 32205482}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-25T18:48:04"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]